# ================================================================ Gen 1:26 Faciamus hominem
# Added 2026-09-05 (Phase 6, part three). Every witness below comments on Gen 1:26, which is
# outside this edition's scripture layer, and is admitted under the SECOND clause of the anchor
# rule (PHASES.md, Frozen conventions): a witness may be anchored on a verse it does not quote
# when it argues the same philological question that verse raises. The question here is the one
# this crux is built on — whether a plural form of speech about God implies a plurality in God —
# and Gen 1:26 is where most of the Latin bench actually argues it. All are anchored on `gen.1.2`
# beside the Elohim material, and each carries a visible ⚠ Anchor note. `data/scripture/` is
# NOT extended: the edition's scope stays Gen 1:1–5 and there is no /dialogue/gen-1-26/ page.
#
# Pruned from the 46-candidate survey in notes/k3-gen-1-26-survey.md, which the reader should
# take as the record of what was left out: Rabanus (PL 107:459C) and Angelomus (PL 115:145A)
# copy Bede verbatim; Rupert's remaining thirty hits are on the image and on predestination, not
# on the plural; Augustine's Confessions XIII (PL 32:858) makes the same plural/singular
# alternation an allegory of the spiritual man rather than an argument about God; and the large
# majority of the 107 raw hits are on ad imaginem, a different question. Bruno's Faciamus
# page came back COVERED by `bruno-gen-1-1c`, built for this crux at Phase 2 — so the Latin
# bench's Gen 1:26 material was already on this daf inside a Gen 1:1 witness, unlabelled.
_ANCHOR26 = ("⚠ Anchor note: this is a comment on Gen 1:26, not on Gen 1:1–5. It is on the daf under "
             "the second clause of the anchor rule — it argues the same philological question the "
             "Elohim of Gen 1:1 raises, whether a plural form of speech about God implies a "
             "plurality in God — and is anchored on Gen 1:2 beside that material. ")

add(id="basil-hex-1-26-judaei", work="basil-hex-lat", author="basil", tradition="greek",
    date=380, date_precision="range-370-380", place="caesarea-cappadociae",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Ferunt enim, quod angelis dixerit: Faciamus hominem", "en": "For they say that he said it to the angels: let us make a human"},
    original=latin("7608", "Et dixit Deus, faciamus hominem. Ubi sunt nunc Judaei",
                   "et periculum de personarum multitudine declinares.", 53),
    english={"text": "And God said, let us make a human. Where are the Jews now, who in what follows — where the light of the Godhead shines as if through certain caverns, and a second person is indeed declared mystically though not yet plainly seen — used to fight back, saying that God was speaking to himself, since he himself said and he himself made, 'let there be light', and there was light? Their absurdities were ready to hand then, as they are now brought out. For what blacksmith, or carpenter, or tanner of hides, standing alone in his workshop with no partner in the craft at hand, says to himself, let us make a sword, or let us put together a plough, or let us shape a shoe — and does not rather begin in silence upon the thing he has settled in his mind? It is empty chatter that any human being should sternly command something to himself, like a judge or a censor. Though what will these men not say, who crucified the Lord, having tongues trained to lying, even though the very voice now present has stopped their mouths altogether? And God said, let us make a human. So long, then, as there was no one to be taught, the preaching of the Godhead lay hidden in the deep. But when the creation of a human began to be looked for, faith was revealed, and the teaching of the truth plainly flashed out. Listen, Jew, you fighter against Christ, how he addresses the partner of the world's making, through whom he made the ages, who does all things by the word of his power: but your mind does not receive the words of piety. For as wild beasts, when they are shut in cages, gnaw at their own wombs with their teeth, showing their own bitterness and yet unable to work off their fury, so the Jew, the adversary of the truth, hemmed in by the straits of these bindings, asserts that there are many persons to whom the Word of God went out, and lies in saying it. For they say that he said it to the angels: let us make a human. A Jewish fiction, truly, and a fabling worthy of their perfidy! So as not to receive one, they confess many; they take the Son into fellowship with servants by a sharing of dignity, and make our fellow slaves the authors of the human form, and want the one who perfected the human to be equalled with angels — when it is absurd that any thing made should be equal to its maker. Let us consider what follows too: let us make a human to our image. What do you answer? Is there one image of God and of the angels? For the Father's must necessarily be the same as the Son's, yet in such a way that the figure is understood as befits God, that is, not consisting in bodily form but in the divine property. Listen, you too, who come forward out of the new circumcision, commending the cult of Judaism under a pretence of Christianity. To whom does he say, to our image, if not to the radiance of the glory and the stamp of the paternal substance, which is the image of the invisible God? He speaks, then, to his own image, which said: I and the Father are one; and, whoever sees me sees my Father also — to this one he says: let us make a human to our image. For where there is a unity of image, nothing can be unlike. For he did not say, to our images, as Scripture also testifies: God made, it says, the human: they did not make. He prudently avoided a multitude of persons here: so that where he confounded the Jew, here he might shut out the rite of the gentiles. Therefore he called to mind the unity, so that you might know the Son to be together with the Father, and might avoid the danger of a multitude of persons.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["not-to-angels", "faciamus-to-the-son", "one-authority-singular-verb"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The most violent page on the site, and the one that proves the two benches were in contact on this question, because it is an answer to an argument the rabbinic bench is on record making. Basil — in Eustathius's Latin, which is how the Latin bench read him — knows three Jewish readings of the plural and takes them in order: that God spoke to himself, which he answers with the workshop analogy (no smith alone says 'let us make a sword'); that God spoke to the angels, which he calls a Jewish fiction and answers from the image (there is not one image of God and of the angels); and, underneath both, that the plural cannot mean persons. Then the sentence that matters most for this daf: Fecit Deus, inquit, hominem: non fecerunt — 'God made the human: they did not make'. That is the singular-verb argument of Bereshit Rabbah 1:7 and of R. Simlai at Bereshit Rabbah 8:9, in Latin, used to the opposite end. The abuse is not incidental and is not softened here: the Jew is a beast gnawing its own womb, the reading is perfidia, and the passage ends by turning on judaizing Christians. It is also, and this is the point for the finding, a Christian text that knows what the other bench says and answers it by name."
)

add(id="ambrose-hex-6-7-40", work="ambrose-hex", author="ambrose", tradition="latin",
    date=387, date_precision="circa", place="milan",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Cui dicit? Non sibi utique, quia non dicit, faciam, sed faciamus", "en": "To whom does he say it? Not to himself, surely, for he does not say 'I shall make' but 'let us make'"},
    original=latin("6958", "Faciamus, inquit, hominem ad nostram imaginem",
                   "Numquid angelis diceret: Faciamus hominem ad nostram imaginem, et ad similitudinem nostram?", 14),
    english={"text": "Let us make, he says, a human to our image and to our likeness (Gen 1:26). Who says this? Is it not God, who made you? What is God? Flesh, or spirit? Not flesh, surely, but spirit, whose likeness flesh cannot be; because he is incorporeal and invisible, whereas flesh is grasped and seen. To whom does he say it? Not to himself, surely, for he does not say, I shall make, but, let us make. Not to angels; because they are servants; and servants with their lord, and works with their author, cannot have a partnership of working: but he says it to the Son, even if the Jews are unwilling, even if the Arians fight back. Let the Jews be silent, and let the Arians be struck dumb along with their parents, who, while they shut one out from the fellowship of the divine working, bring in many; and the prerogative they deny to the Son they make a gift of to little servants. But be it so — that God should seem to you to have needed the support of little servants in order to work. If the working is common to God with the angels, is the image then common to God and the angels? Would he say to angels: let us make a human to our image and to our likeness?", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["not-to-angels", "faciamus-to-the-son"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Ambrose puts the question in the sharpest possible form — cui dicit?, to whom does he say it? — and works through the same three candidates as Basil, whom he is reading, in the same order: not to himself, because the verb is not faciam; not to the angels, because a servant is no partner in the master's work; therefore to the Son. What he adds to Basil is the polemical pairing: the Jews and the Arians are refuted in one breath, and by the same argument, that whoever denies the Son a share in the working has to give that share to somebody, and ends by handing it to servuli, little servants. The rabbinic bench's own answer — that God took counsel with the household above — is thus not merely rejected on this daf but is treated as the position a Christian falls into by mistake if he gets the Son wrong. Note also that the Latin argument turns on a Latin fact: faciam against faciamus is exactly the alternative Rashi weighs at Troyes seven hundred years later, in Hebrew, when he writes that had it said e'eseh adam we could not have learned that God speaks with his court."
)

add(id="aug-gnl-imp-16-61", work="aug-gnl-imp", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=393, date_precision="circa", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "a qua pluralitate Spiritum sanctum separare quis audeat?", "en": "who would dare to separate the Holy Spirit from that plurality?"},
    original=latin("7310", "Ille autem sensus est potius in his divinis verbis eligendos",
                   "Fecit Deus hominem ad imaginem Dei, tanquam diceretur ad imaginem suam, quod est ipsa Trinitas.", 34),
    english={"text": "But that sense is rather to be chosen in these divine words, that we should understand it as said not in the singular but in the plural — let us make a human to our image and likeness — because the human was made not to the image of the Father alone, or of the Son alone, or of the Holy Spirit alone, but to the image of the Trinity itself. Which Trinity is so a Trinity that it is one God; is so one God that it is a Trinity. For he does not say, speaking to the Son, let us make a human to your image, or, to my image: but he says in the plural, to our image and likeness: and who would dare to separate the Holy Spirit from that plurality? And since that plurality is not three gods but one God, it is therefore to be understood that Scripture afterwards brought it in in the singular, and said, and God made the human to the image of God; so that it should not be taken as though God the Father made him to the image of God, that is, of his Son: otherwise how is it true, what was said, to our image, if the human was made to the image of the Son alone? And by this, since what God says is true, to our image, it was said, God made the human to the image of God, as though it were said, to his own image, which is the Trinity itself.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["image-of-the-whole-trinity", "faciamus-is-the-trinity"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Augustine refuses the reading Ambrose and Basil had settled on, and he does it on grammatical grounds. If the Father is speaking to the Son, then ad imaginem nostram would have to mean 'to your image' or 'to my image', and it says neither; and if the plural is the Father and the Son only, who would dare to shut the Holy Spirit out of it? So the plural is neither a conversation nor a plural of two: it is the Trinity speaking about itself, and the human is made to the image of the whole. Then the second half, which is the move the whole Latin bench inherits: the plural of v. 26 and the singular of v. 27 are read as one argument, the plural giving the persons and the singular the one God. That pairing is the exact formal structure of R. Simlai's answer to the minim at Bereshit Rabbah 8:9 — let us make is met with and he created, not and they created — with the conclusion reversed. Augustine is not answering the midrash and cannot have known it; he has arrived at the same reading of the same two verses as a pair, and taken the plural to be real."
)

add(id="aug-gnl-3-19-29", work="aug-gnl", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=401, date_precision="range-401-415", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "ad insinuandam pluralitatem personarum", "en": "to intimate a plurality of persons"},
    original=latin("7302", "hoc primum breviter dicimus, non indifferenter accipiendum",
                   "unum Deum accipiamus, propter quod dictum est, ad imaginem Dei.", 34),
    english={"text": "This first we say briefly, that it is not to be taken as a matter of indifference that in the other works it is said, God said, let it be; but here, God said, let us make a human to our image and likeness: namely, to intimate, if I may so put it, a plurality of persons on account of the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit. Yet he at once warns us that the unity of the deity is to be understood, saying, and God made the human to the image of God; not as though the Father to the image of the Son, or the Son to the image of the Father; otherwise it was not truly said, to our image, if the human was made to the image of the Father alone, or of the Son alone: but it was said thus, God made to the image of God, as though it were said, God made to his own image. And since it now says, to the image of God, when it had said above, to our image, it is signified that that plurality of persons does not work so that we should either say, or believe, or understand many gods; but that we should take the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit — on account of which Trinity it was said, to our image — as one God, on account of which it was said, to the image of God.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity", "image-of-the-whole-trinity"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The mature statement of the same reading, twenty years after the unfinished commentary, and the sentence the twelfth century will read as the settled Latin answer: the change of formula is not indifferent. Everywhere else dixit Deus, fiat; here dixit Deus, faciamus — and the difference is there to intimate a plurality of persons. Augustine's whole argument is an argument from the shape of the sentence and from nothing else: he has no Hebrew, no grammatical claim about the verb, and no interest in what the word for God is. The observation that the formula changes at this one verse is, on the other bench, R. Yeshoshua's and R. Ami's question at Bereshit Rabbah 8:3 — be-mi nimlakh, with whom did he take counsel — and Rupert of Deutz will state it as mutavit vocem suam, he changed his voice. It is this passage, and not the Confessions, that the Glossa prints on Gen 1:26 under Augustine's name, so it is the form in which the schoolroom of 1150 receives the question."
)

add(id="aug-civ-16-6", work="aug-civ", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=417, date_precision="range-413-427", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Qualis intelligenda sit esse locutio, qua Deus Angelis loquitur", "en": "What kind of speech is to be understood, by which God speaks to the angels"},
    original=latin("21364", "Poterat et illud, quando factus est homo, de Angelis intelligi",
                   "tanquam lex aeterna in illa eorum curia superna.", 41),
    english={"text": "That too, when the human was made, could have been understood of the angels — what was said, let us make a human, since he did not say, I shall make: but because there follows, to our image; and it is not lawful to believe that the human was made to the image of the angels, or that the image of the angels and of God is the same; therefore the plurality of the Trinity is rightly understood there. Which Trinity, however, because it is one God, even when he had said, let us make, it says, and God made the human to the image of God: he did not say, the gods made, or, to the image of the gods. Here too the same Trinity could be understood, as though the Father had said to the Son and the Holy Spirit, come, and going down let us confound their language there — if there were anything to prevent the angels being understood: to whom it rather belongs to come to God by holy motions, that is, by pious thoughts, by which the unchangeable Truth is consulted by them, as an eternal law in that supernal court of theirs.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["not-to-angels", "faciamus-is-the-trinity", "one-authority-singular-verb"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The chapter is headed what kind of speech is to be understood, by which God speaks to the angels, and it is the closest the two benches come to holding the same page open. Augustine treats Gen 1:26 and Gen 11:7 — venite, et descendentes confundamus ibi linguam eorum — as one problem with one shape: both are plural, both could be addressed to angels, and each has to be decided on its own evidence. At Gen 1:26 he decides against the angels, because of ad imaginem nostram; at Gen 11:7, where there is no image clause, he grants that nothing prevents the angels being understood, and takes it that way. Now the other bench pairs the same two verses. R. Yoḥanan at b. Sanhedrin 38b lists the places where the minim found an opening and the refutation beside each: 'let us make a human in our image' answered by 'and God created the human in his image', and 'come, let us go down and confound their language' answered by 'and the LORD came down to see the city and the tower'. The same two verses, chosen for the same reason — a plural verb of divine speech — and set out as a pair. Augustine is refuting the angel reading of the first and accepting it for the second; R. Yoḥanan is refuting a plurality of makers in both. Neither knows the other. The pairing is not a topic they share but a structure the Hebrew text itself imposes, and both benches found it."
)

add(id="bede-gen-1-26", work="bede-gen", author="bede", tradition="latin",
    date=720, date_precision="circa", place="jarrow",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "In eo enim quod dicitur Faciamus, una ostenditur trium personarum operatio", "en": "For in the fact that it says 'let us make', one working of three persons is shown"},
    original=latin("8466", "Cum autem dicitur faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram, unitas sanctae Trinitatis",
                   "unam esse eamdemque imaginem sive similitudinem credamus.", 91),
    english={"text": "But when it is said, let us make a human to our image and likeness, the unity of the holy Trinity is openly commended. For indeed the same undivided Trinity had been mystically intimated in the preceding forming of things, when it was said: and God said, let it be; and God made; and God saw that it was good. Now, however, this same thing is intimated more plainly, when it is said: let us make a human to our image and likeness — and rightly, because so long as there was no one who might be taught, the preaching of the Godhead lay hidden in the deep; but when the creation of a human began to be looked for, faith was revealed, and the teaching of truth plainly flashed out. For in the fact that it says, let us make, one working of three persons is shown; and in what follows, to our image and likeness, the one and equal substance of that same holy Trinity is indicated. For how would the image and likeness be one, if the Son were less than the Father, if the Holy Spirit were less than the Son, if the glory of the whole Trinity were not consubstantial and of the same power? Or how would it be said, let us make, if the cooperating power of three persons in one deity did not exist? For it could not be said to angels by God, let us make a human to our image and likeness, since no reasoning whatever allows us to believe that the image and likeness of God and of the angels is one and the same.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity", "not-to-angels", "trinity-in-the-formula"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Bede takes the whole question over from the Latin Basil — 'so long as there was no one who might be taught, the preaching of the Godhead lay hidden in the deep' is Eustathius's sentence word for word — and turns a polemic into a lesson. The Jews are gone; what is left is a clean division of the verse into two proofs: faciamus gives one working of three persons, ad imaginem nostram gives one substance, and the angels are excluded by a ratio rather than by abuse. He also does something Augustine does not, and it is Rupert's argument three centuries early: the Trinity was already intimated mystically in the threefold formula of the earlier days — God said, God made, God saw that it was good — and is intimated more plainly here. This is the version that travels. Rabanus copies it verbatim at Fulda (PL 107:459C) and Angelomus at Luxeuil (PL 115:145A); neither is on this daf, because neither adds a word."
)

add(id="alcuin-int-36-37", work="alcuin-int", author="alcuin", tradition="latin",
    date=796, date_precision="circa", place="tours",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Cur plurali numero dixit: Faciamus?", "en": "Why did he say it in the plural number: let us make?"},
    original=latin("21416", "Inter. 36. Quare de solo homine dictum est: Faciamus hominem",
                   "Resp. Ut ostenderetur trium una operatio personarum.", 100),
    english={"text": "Question 36. Why is it said of the human alone: let us make a human (Gen 1:26); whereas of the other creatures we read: God said? — Answer. So that, since a rational creature was being founded, it might be seen to be made with counsel, and so that its nobility might be shown. Question 37. Why did he say it in the plural number: let us make? — Answer. So that one working of three persons might be shown.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity", "dignity-of-the-creature"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The whole crux reduced to two questions and twenty-eight words of answer, which is what the Interrogationes are for, and worth having on the daf precisely because it shows what the Carolingian school thought a pupil needed. Two distinct answers are given and neither cancels the other: the consultative form marks the dignity of the rational creature, and the plural number shows one working of three persons. Note that the first of these is the only thing Alcuin says about the change of formula, and it is not a Trinitarian point at all — it is an argument about the honour of the thing made, which is Bereshit Rabbah 8:8's argument for why the plural was worth the risk (Moses is told to write it anyway, so that the great should learn to consult the small) with the moral swapped from humility to nobility. Four questions later, at Inter. 40, Alcuin returns to it and gives the Augustinian pairing — cur iterum dixit: Creavit Deus hominem ad imaginem suam, cum antea dixisset: ad imaginem nostram? … ut utrumque, et pluralitas personarum, et unitas substantiae insinuaretur — but that is a distinct argument and is not merged into this slice. ⛔ Alcuin does not answer a lemma grep on this crux's Latin words: the Interrogationes are organised as questions and the lemma sits in the question, not in a running exposition."
)

add(id="wigbod-gen-1-26", work="wigbod-gen", author="wigbod", tradition="latin",
    date=790, date_precision="circa", place="tours",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "D. … M. Ad insinuandum pluralitatem personarum", "en": "D. … M. To intimate a plurality of persons"},
    original=latin("8606", "D. Hoc primum breviter dicimus non indifferenter accipiendum",
                   "Et fecit Deus hominem ad imaginem suam.", 96),
    english={"text": "D. This first we say briefly, that it is not to be taken as a matter of indifference that in the other works it is said, God said, let it be; but here, God said, let us make. M. To intimate, if I may so put it, a plurality of persons, on account of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit — yet he at once warned that the unity of the deity is to be understood, saying: and God made the human to his own image.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Augustine's sentence from De Genesi ad litteram III.19 cut in half and given to two speakers, D and M, so that the observation becomes a pupil's and the doctrine a master's. Nothing is added and one thing is lost: Augustine wrote ad imaginem Dei, and had a paragraph of argument about why it is not ad imaginem Filii; Wigbod writes ad imaginem suam, which makes the point unarguable and unexaminable at once. The compilation is also where the attribution slips — the excerpt is headed AMBROSIUS, de creatione hominis, and the words are Augustine's. That is the ordinary condition of the Carolingian florilegia and is why the Latin bench of the ninth century can be read for what it circulated rather than for what it thought."
)

add(id="remigius-gen-1-26", work="remigius-gen", author="remigius", tradition="latin",
    date=890, date_precision="circa", place="auxerre",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "quasi quodam concilio dicit: Faciamus", "en": "as though by a kind of council he says: let us make"},
    original=latin("9346", "Vers. 26. Faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram. Cum caetera jubendo fecerit",
                   "melius tamen ad sanctam Trinitatem refertur.", 131),
    english={"text": "Verse 26. Let us make a human to our image and likeness. Whereas he made the rest by commanding, God — as though about to make the greatest thing, and one to be preferred to all that he had made — says, as though by a kind of council: let us make. And although some say that this is to be referred to the angels, it is better referred to the holy Trinity.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity", "dignity-of-the-creature", "faciamus-to-the-angels"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Three sentences, and the middle one is the most useful thing on the Latin half of this daf. Quasi quodam concilio dicit: Faciamus — as though by a kind of council he says, let us make — is Bereshit Rabbah 8:3's be-mi nimlakh, with whom did he take counsel, in a Latin word; and the third sentence records, without naming anyone, that some say it is to be referred to the angels. That is the rabbinic bench's own primary answer, reported at Auxerre about 890 as a live opinion among Latin readers and rejected only comparatively — melius tamen, it is better referred to the Trinity, not: it is impossible. Remigius is the one Latin witness here who neither refutes the angel reading nor accepts it, and the mildness is the evidence: by the end of the ninth century the reading is in the schools as an option, and nobody on this daf says where it came from."
)

add(id="glossa-1-26", work="glossa", author="glossa-ordinaria", tradition="latin",
    date=1120, date_precision="compilation-1110-1130", place="laon",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Insinuatur pluralitas personarum Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus sancti", "en": "A plurality of persons is intimated — of the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit"},
    original=latin("8950", "VERS. 26.-- « Faciamus hominem. » (AUG., ibid., c. 19.) Insinuatur pluralitas personarum",
                   "sed ut Patrem, et Filium, et Spiritum sanctum unum Deum accipiamus.", 113),
    english={"text": "Verse 26. 'Let us make a human.' (AUG., ibid., c. 19.) A plurality of persons is intimated — of the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit — and at once the unity of the deity, when it is said: 'and God made the human to the image of God', not the Father to the image only of the Son. For it would not truly be said, to our image; but it was said thus: 'God made the human to the image of God', as if it were said 'to his own image'. And when it says 'to the image of God', when above it had said 'to our image', it is signified that the plurality of persons does not work so that we should believe in many gods, but so that we should take the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit as one God.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity", "image-of-the-whole-trinity"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The school answer in the form the twelfth century actually read: Augustine's De Genesi ad litteram III.19, printed under his name, abridged to the two moves that matter and set beside the verse. What the abridgement drops is the argument — Augustine's careful 'otherwise how is it true, what was said, to our image, if the human was made to the image of the Son alone?' becomes the flat 'for it would not truly be said, to our image' — so that a reader of the Gloss gets the conclusion with the reasoning compressed to a clause. This is the page on which Peter Comestor, Hugh and Abelard all begin. The same volume of the Gloss, ninety columns later, prints the angel reading of Gen 11:7 without a word of warning; see `glossa-11-7-angeli`."
)

add(id="glossa-11-7-angeli", work="glossa", author="glossa-ordinaria", tradition="latin",
    date=1120, date_precision="compilation-1110-1130", place="laon",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Ad angelos dixisse intelligitur", "en": "He is understood to have said it to the angels"},
    original=latin("8950", "VERS. 7.-- « Venite igitur, » etc. Ad angelos dixisse intelligitur",
                   "sicut ibi: « Fecit Deus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem suam. »", 113),
    english={"text": "Verse 7. 'Come, therefore', etc. He is understood to have said it to the angels; or according to that: 'let us make a human to our image and likeness.' For it is added in the singular number: 'and he divided them', etc., just as there: 'God made the human to his own image and likeness.'", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-to-the-angels", "one-authority-singular-verb"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "⚠ A second anchor note applies: this is a gloss on Gen 11:7, the tower of Babel, not on Gen 1:26 or on Gen 1:1–5. It is on the daf because it says of venite, confundamus what nobody in the Gloss says of faciamus: he is understood to have said it to the angels. And it gets there by the same route the rabbinic bench does. The gloss pairs Gen 11:7 with Gen 1:26 as the two plural verses; it then answers the plural of Gen 11:7 with the singular of Gen 11:8 — 'and he divided them' — exactly as the plural of Gen 1:26 is answered by the singular of Gen 1:27. That is R. Yoḥanan's rule at b. Sanhedrin 38b, which lists the same two verses in the same order with the same singular refutations beside them, and it is the structure of Augustine's chapter de locutione qua Deus Angelis loquitur (`aug-civ-16-6`), where Gen 1:26 is refused to the angels and Gen 11:7 is granted to them. Three benches, the same pair of verses, the same test. The Gloss is the one place where a Latin schoolbook prints the angel reading as the plain sense, and it does so where the image clause is missing and the argument against it therefore fails."
)

add(id="rupert-gen-1-26-consilium", work="rupert-gen", author="rupert", tradition="latin",
    date=1117, date_precision="range-1112-1117", place="liege",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "non tam senatu quam soliloquio venerando", "en": "not so much a senate as a venerable soliloquy"},
    original=latin("10873", "Ait: Faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram. Mutavit vocem suam",
                   "non tam senatu quam soliloquio venerando de nobis peccatoribus habitum est.", 167),
    english={"text": "He says: let us make a human to our image and likeness. He changed his voice — he who until now had said of each single thing: let it be, and it was made; let the earth bring forth, let the waters bring forth, and it was made. But now, as though he had sat down wearied from the circuit of earth and heaven and sea, and while catching his breath it had come into his mind that one thing was still lacking which it would be fitting or needful to make: let us make, he says, a human to our image and likeness. Lord, says the prophet, let your ancient counsel be made true. Or does the counsel seem to you a small one, to have been signified by these few little sayings of the holy Trinity? A plainly great counsel was held in that counsel of Wisdom, in that — not so much a senate as a venerable soliloquy — of such persons, the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit, concerning us sinners.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal", "spiritual"],
    answers=["faciamus-is-the-trinity", "consilium-not-senate"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Rupert states the philological observation more plainly than anyone else on the Latin bench — mutavit vocem suam, he changed his voice — and then does something no one else does: he names the two things the change of voice could mean and rejects one of them. The counsel is non tam senatu quam soliloquio, not so much a senate as a soliloquy. That is the disagreement of Bereshit Rabbah 8:3 settled in four words, and settled the other way from where it starts. There R. Yehoshua in R. Levi's name says God took counsel with the work of heaven and earth, and the parable is of a king with two sanqlitin — Greek synklētikoi, senators — while R. Ami says be-libbo nimlakh, he took counsel with his own heart. Rupert, who has never heard of either, uses the Roman word for the first and the Augustinian word for the second, and chooses the second. Abelard chooses it too, twenty years later, and gives it a source: a man consulting his own reason makes two of himself, as in Boethius and in Augustine's Soliloquies. The image of God pausing for breath is Rupert's own and is not in the tradition."
)

add(id="abelard-hex-1-26-faciamus", work="abelard-hex", author="abelard", tradition="latin",
    date=1133, date_precision="circa", place="paraclete",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Sed cur pluraliter dicitur: faciamus hominem ad imaginem nostram, si nulla prorsus pluralitas in Deo sit", "en": "But why is it said in the plural — let us make a human to our image — if there is no plurality at all in God?"},
    original=latin("11118", "Cujus quidem creationis excellentiam patenter insinuat",
                   "Faciamus eum talem et tantum, ut imago nostra sit vel similitudo.", 178),
    english={"text": "The excellence of this creation he plainly intimates, when, speaking as though out of a kind of counsel, he says: let us make. But why is it said in the plural — let us make a human to our image — if there is no plurality at all in God, who alone is afterwards recorded to have created the human to his own image in these words: and God created the human to his own image, to the image of God he created him? Let the Jews say, if they can, or else confess with us that in the one essence of the Divinity there is a plurality of persons rather than of things. Considering this carefully, the prophet says in the plural, in order to make a distinction of persons: let us make; and in order to assign the unity of God he added in the singular: and God created the human, etc. So, then, as someone speaking with himself sets up himself and his own reason as though they were two when he consults it — as Boethius does in the book *On the Consolation of Philosophy*, or Augustine in the book of *Soliloquies* — so God the Father, as though inviting both Wisdom and Goodness, that is the Son and the Holy Spirit, to the creation of the human, says: let us make him such and so great, that he may be our image or likeness.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["elohim-is-plural-of-persons", "faciamus-is-the-trinity", "consilium-not-senate", "one-authority-singular-verb"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The same man who is this crux's star witness on Eloim at Gen 1:1, asking the same question at the other verse, and the two pages should be read together: at 1:1 the evidence is a Hebrew noun that is plural in form beside a singular verb, and here it is a Latin verb that is plural in form beside a singular verb three lines later. Both times the argument runs the same way — the plural gives the persons, the singular gives the one creator — and both times it is addressed to Jews by name: dicant Judaei si possunt. What is new here is the analogy, and it is the most interesting thing on the Latin bench for this daf. A man who consults his own reason 'sets up himself and his reason as though they were two', as Boethius does in the Consolation and Augustine in the Soliloquies. That is R. Ami's answer at Bereshit Rabbah 8:3, be-libbo nimlakh — he took counsel with his heart — used to prove the opposite conclusion: for R. Ami the inward counsel shows that no second party was involved, for Abelard it shows how a real plurality can speak in one mouth. Rupert had reached the same figure sixteen years earlier and called it a soliloquy without naming the book."
)

add(id="hugh-adnot-1-26", work="hugh-adnot", author="hugh-of-st-victor", tradition="latin",
    date=1130, date_precision="circa", place="paris",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "cum ipse ad angelos ita loquatur, quorum ministerio forsitan formatum est corpus hominis", "en": "since he himself so speaks to the angels, by whose ministry perhaps the human body was formed"},
    original=latin("11054", "Faciamus hominem ad imaginem, et similitudinem nostram. Primo dixit: In principio creavit Deus coelum et terram",
                   "per hoc quod subjungit singulariter ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram, unitatem essentiae.", 175),
    english={"text": "Let us make a human to our image and likeness. First he said: in the beginning God created heaven and earth. Then he said: let there be light; here, thirdly, he adds: let us make a human, etc. In this three advances may be named. First the work, then the informing word, thirdly the counsel — by this, that he here adds: let us make a human, etc. Nor does he bring in counsel because he could not equally well make both great things and small; but in order to show the dignity of the human that was created, and to make us careful not to disdain to take counsel both from equals and from lesser people — since he himself so speaks to the angels, by whose ministry perhaps the human body was formed. Or, what is better, let us take it that it was the counsel of the Trinity, and that by the plural word there is a distinction of persons; and by this, that he adds in the singular, to our image and likeness, a unity of essence.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal", "spiritual"],
    answers=["faciamus-to-the-angels", "faciamus-is-the-trinity", "dignity-of-the-creature"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The one place on the Latin bench where the angel reading is not merely reported but taught, and taught as a moral. Hugh gives the change of formula a shape — work, informing word, counsel, three advances across Gen 1:1, 1:3 and 1:26 — and then draws from the third the lesson that we should not disdain to take counsel from equals and from lesser people, since he himself so speaks to the angels. He goes further than any rabbinic witness on this daf dares to: quorum ministerio forsitan formatum est corpus hominis, by whose ministry perhaps the human body was formed, which is the thing Basil calls a Jewish fiction and Bruno says would make many creators. Only then comes vel quod melius est, or what is better — the counsel of the Trinity. So the school of St Victor about 1130 holds both readings, prefers the second, and gets from the first exactly the moral Bereshit Rabbah 8:8 gets: that the great should consult the small, and that the Creator did it first. Hugh's ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram is called singular in the last clause; he means the singular of Gen 1:27, and the sentence is loose in the printed text."
)

add(id="comestor-hs-1-26", work="comestor-hs", author="comestor", tradition="latin",
    date=1170, date_precision="range-1169-1173", place="paris",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"la": "Et loquitur Pater ad Filium, et Spiritum sanctum", "en": "And the Father speaks to the Son and the Holy Spirit"},
    original=latin("11575", "Deinde subditur de creatione hominis sic: Faciamus hominem",
                   "factus est autem homo ad imaginem Dei, quantum ad animam.", 198),
    english={"text": "Then it is added concerning the creation of the human thus: let us make a human, etc. (Gen 1). And the Father speaks to the Son and the Holy Spirit. Or it is as it were the common voice of the three persons, 'let us make', and 'our': and the human was made to the image of God, as regards the soul.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["faciamus-to-the-son", "faciamus-is-the-trinity"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The handbook settling it, and the interest is in what it settles and what it leaves standing. Comestor gives two readings without ranking them: the Father speaks to the Son and the Holy Spirit — which is Ambrose's and Basil's answer, refused by Augustine on the ground that ad imaginem nostram would then have to be tuam or meam — or the plural is the common voice of the three, which is Augustine's. He does not notice that the first is the one Augustine wrote two books to exclude, and neither reading is argued for. The angels are not mentioned at all: between Hugh at St Victor about 1130, who teaches the angel reading as a moral and grants the angels a possible ministry in forming the body, and the Historia scholastica about 1170, which is the book every priest in Latin Europe will be examined on, the option has simply dropped out of the syllabus."
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- rabbinic bench on Gen 1:26
# ⛔ The Bereshit Rabbah Hebrew for chapter 8 is NOT from the Wikisource version used for chapters
# 1-3, which does not cover chapter 8 at all. It is "Daat Bereshit Rabbah" (Public Domain), which
# is the version that failed for chapters 1-3. So the BR Hebrew in this edition comes from two
# versions by chapter, and the colophon says so.
_BR8_SRC = {"license": "daat-br", "version": "Sefaria 'Daat Bereshit Rabbah' (Public Domain). Chapter 8 is not in 'Wikisource Bereshit Rabbah', the version used for chapters 1-3"}

add(id="br-8-3", work="br", author="bereshit-rabbah", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=450, date_precision="compilation-400-500", place="galilee",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים נַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם. בְּמִי נִמְלַךְ?", "en": "'And God said, let us make a human' — with whom did he take counsel?"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": _raw("br-8.json", "he", 2)[0], "source": "Bereshit Rabbah 8:3 (Vilna numbering)", **_BR8_SRC},
    english={"text": _raw("br-8.json", "en", 2)[0], **_BR_EN},
    tradents=["r-yehoshua-of-sikhnin", "r-levi", "r-shmuel-b-nachman", "r-ami", "rav-asi"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["counsel-with-the-household-above", "consilium-not-senate"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The rabbinic bench asks the Latin bench's question in three words — be-mi nimlakh, with whom did he take counsel — and then, instead of answering it, prints four answers side by side and lets them stand. R. Yehoshua in R. Levi's name: he took counsel with the work of heaven and earth, and the parable is of a king with two sanqlitin, the Greek synklētikoi, senators, without whose knowledge he did nothing. R. Shmuel bar Naḥman: with the work of each day, like a king with one counsellor. R. Ami: be-libbo nimlakh, he took counsel with his own heart — and the parables that follow are about blame rather than about counsel, the architect and the broker who lose the king's money, so that 'he was saddened in his heart' (Gen 6:6) explains the counsel backwards from the regret. Rav Asi repeats the second. Note what is not here and is in every Latin witness on this daf: nobody suggests that the plural could point to a plurality in God. The question is whom God consulted, not who spoke; and the two extremes the midrash offers, a senate and a soliloquy, are the two Rupert of Deutz will name in Latin at Liège six hundred years later — non tam senatu quam soliloquio — choosing the soliloquy, as R. Ami does, and meaning by it the opposite thing."
)

add(id="br-8-8", work="br", author="bereshit-rabbah", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=450, date_precision="compilation-400-500", place="galilee",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "רִבּוֹן הָעוֹלָם מָה אַתָּה נוֹתֵן פִּתְחוֹן פֶּה לַמִּינִים", "en": "Master of the world, why are you giving the minim an opening?"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": _raw("br-8.json", "he", 7)[0], "source": "Bereshit Rabbah 8:8 (Vilna numbering)", **_BR8_SRC},
    english={"text": _raw("br-8.json", "en", 7)[0], **_BR_EN},
    tradents=["r-shmuel-b-nachman", "r-yonatan", "r-levi"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["counsel-with-the-household-above", "against-the-heretics", "dignity-of-the-creature"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The single most important passage on this daf, because it is the rabbinic bench saying out loud that it knows what the other bench does with this verse. Moses is writing the Torah at dictation; he reaches na'aseh adam and stops: ribbon ha-olam, mah attah noten pitchon peh la-minim — Master of the world, why are you giving the minim an opening? And the answer is not a defence of the plural but a refusal to protect it: 'write, and whoever wants to err, let him err.' Then the reason the risk was worth taking, and it is a moral one: God made greater and lesser, and a great man who will not ask leave of a lesser is to learn from his Creator, who consulted the ministering angels before making a human. R. Levi will not have even that — let hakha malkhu, there is no consulting here — and gives a parable in which the king asks what to do with a block of stone, hears his advisers say baths, and makes a statue: who is going to stop him? So the passage holds three positions at once: that the plural is dangerous, that it teaches humility, and that the consultation was never real. What it does not do is deny that the verse can be read as the minim read it. That is the concession the Latin bench never makes and never needs to, and it is the clearest evidence on the site that the two readings of Gen 1:26 were known to each other."
)

_br89_he = _raw("br-8.json", "he", 8)[0]
_br89_en = _raw("br-8.json", "en", 8)[0]
add(id="br-8-9", work="br", author="bereshit-rabbah", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=450, date_precision="compilation-400-500", place="galilee",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "שָׁאֲלוּ הַמִּינִים אֶת רַבִּי שִׂמְלַאי: כַּמָּה אֱלֹהוּת בָּרְאוּ אֶת הָעוֹלָם?", "en": "The minim asked Rabbi Simlai: how many divinities created the world?"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": _br89_he, "source": "Bereshit Rabbah 8:9 (Vilna numbering)", **_BR8_SRC},
    english={"text": ecut(_br89_en, "The heretics once asked Rabbi Samlai",
                         "nor both of them without the Divine Presence.’", "K3 br-8-9 en"), **_BR_EN},
    tradents=["r-simlai"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["one-authority-singular-verb", "refutation-beside-the-claim", "against-the-heretics"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "A disputation, reported as a disputation, on exactly the two verses this crux is built on. The minim ask R. Simlai how many divinities created the world; he answers from Deut 4:32, bara and not baru; they come back with Gen 1:1, and he gives the same answer — baru Elohim is not written here but bara Elohim, which is R. Yitzḥak's argument at Bereshit Rabbah 1:7 word for word (see `br-1-7`); they come back with na'aseh adam be-tzalmenu, and he sends them to the next verse, va-yivra and not va-yivre'u. Between the second and third exchange he states the rule the whole passage exists to illustrate: wherever you find an opening for the minim, you find the refutation beside it. Three things follow for this daf. The singular-verb argument that K1 and this crux both carry as a piece of grammar is here a polemical instrument aimed at named opponents. Gen 1:1 and Gen 1:26 are treated as one question, which is the reason this Gen 1:26 material belongs on a Gen 1:1–2 daf at all. And the disciples are not satisfied — those men you pushed off with a reed; what will you answer us? — so the midrash itself marks the grammatical answer as a debating move rather than an explanation, and gives the students a different one: that from now on the image needs a man, a woman and the Shekhinah. […] The passage continues in the printed English with two further exchanges, on Josh 22:22 and Josh 24:19, which are the same manoeuvre on other verses; they are outside the Hebrew segment as Daat prints it and are elided here."
)

_sanh3 = " ".join(_raw("b-sanh-38b.json", "he", i)[0] for i in (3, 4))
add(id="b-sanh-38b-malachim", work="bavli-sanhedrin", author="bavli", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=550, date_precision="redaction-500-600", place="babylonia",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "רְצוֹנְכֶם נַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם בְּצַלְמֵנוּ", "en": "Is it your will that we make a human in our image?"},
    original={"lang": "arc", "text": hcut(_sanh3, "אמר רב יהודה", "כל מה שאתה רוצה לעשות בעולמך עשה", "K3 sanh-38b-malachim"),
              "source": "b. Sanhedrin 38b (Vilna)", "license": "cc-by-sa", "version": _raw("b-sanh-38b.json", "he")[1]},
    english={"text": "Rav Yehuda said, Rav said: at the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, sought to create the human, he created one company of ministering angels. He said to them: is it your will that we make a human in our image? They said before him: Master of the world, what are his deeds? He said to them: such and such are his deeds. They said before him: Master of the world, 'what is a mortal, that you remember him, and a human being, that you take account of him?' (Ps 8:5). He put out his little finger among them and burned them. And so with the second company. The third company said before him: Master of the world, the ones who spoke before you first — what did they achieve? The whole world is yours; whatever you wish to do in your world, do.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=["rav-yehuda", "rav"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["counsel-with-the-household-above"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The angel reading in its most literal and least apologetic form: God does not merely take counsel with the ministering angels, he puts the question to them in the words of the verse — retzonkhem na'aseh adam be-tzalmenu, is it your will that we make a human in our image — and when they object he burns them, twice, until a third company learns not to answer. Two things make this the sharpest rabbinic witness here. First, the plural of the verse is taken as a real address to real hearers, which is precisely the reading Basil calls a Jewish fiction, Ambrose refuses because a servant cannot be a partner in the work, Bede excludes by a ratio, and Bruno says would produce many creators; the Latin bench's characterisation of the position is, on this page, accurate. Second, the story concedes the objection the Latin bench presses: the angels contribute nothing, are destroyed for speaking, and are told the world is not theirs. The consultation is real and the partnership is not, which is a distinction no Latin witness on this daf considers."
)

add(id="b-sanh-38b-tshuva", work="bavli-sanhedrin", author="bavli", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=550, date_precision="redaction-500-600", place="babylonia",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "כָּל מָקוֹם שֶׁפָּקְרוּ הַמִּינִים תְּשׁוּבָתָן בְּצִידָּן", "en": "Everywhere the minim blasphemed, their refutation is beside them"},
    original={"lang": "arc", "text": _raw("b-sanh-38b.json", "he", 13)[0], "source": "b. Sanhedrin 38b (Vilna)", "license": "cc-by-sa", "version": _raw("b-sanh-38b.json", "he")[1]},
    english={"text": "Rabbi Yoḥanan said: everywhere the minim blasphemed, their refutation is beside them. 'Let us make a human in our image' (Gen 1:26) — and it says, 'and God created the human in his image' (Gen 1:27). 'Come, let us go down and confound their language there' (Gen 11:7) — 'and the LORD came down to see the city and the tower' (Gen 11:5). 'For there the divine beings were revealed to him' (Gen 35:7) — 'to the God who answers me in the day of my distress' (Gen 35:3).", **DRAFT},
    tradents=["r-yochanan"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["refutation-beside-the-claim", "one-authority-singular-verb", "against-the-heretics"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The rule of R. Simlai at Bereshit Rabbah 8:9 stated as a rule and then run down a list, and the list is what matters here. Three verses where God speaks or is spoken of in the plural, each with the singular that follows it set beside it as its own answer: na'aseh adam against va-yivra, havah nerdah at Babel against va-yered YHWH, niglu elav ha-Elohim against la-El ha-oneh oti. The first two are exactly the pair Augustine takes up at De civitate Dei XVI.6 in a chapter headed what kind of speech is to be understood, by which God speaks to the angels, where Gen 1:26 is refused to the angels because of the image clause and Gen 11:7 is granted to them because there is nothing to prevent it; and they are exactly the pair the Glossa Ordinaria joins at PL 113:114D, glossing venite at Babel by faciamus hominem and answering both with their singulars. Three benches select the same two verses out of the whole Pentateuch and apply the same test to them. Nobody is reading anybody. The selection is forced by the Hebrew, and the disagreement is only about what the plural was doing there in the first place. Note the word: pakru, they blasphemed or broke loose — a harder verb than the she'elu, they asked, of the midrash's version, and the Bavli has dropped the disputation and kept the rule."
)

add(id="rashi-1-26-anvetanuto", work="rashi-gen", author="rashi", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=1090, date_precision="range-1075-1105", place="troyes",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "עַנְוְתָנוּתוֹ שֶׁל הַקָּבָּ\"ה לָמַדְנוּ מִכָּאן", "en": "The humility of the Holy One, blessed be He, we learn from here"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": _raw("rashi-gen-1-26.json", "he", 0)[0], "source": "Rashi on Gen 1:26, s.v. נעשה אדם (first comment)", "license": "silbermann", "version": "Rosenbaum–Silbermann, 1929–34"},
    english={"text": _raw("rashi-gen-1-26.json", "en", 0)[0], "translator": "M. Rosenbaum and A. M. Silbermann, 1929–34", "license": "silbermann"},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["counsel-with-the-household-above"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Rashi takes the angel reading as the plain sense and gives it a motive: anvetanuto shel ha-Qadosh barukh Hu, the humility of the Holy One, learned from this verse. Because the human is in the likeness of the angels and they might be envious, he took counsel with them; and this is his habit, for Micaiah saw the LORD on his throne with all the host of heaven standing to his right and his left (1 Kgs 22:19), which cannot be a right and a left but is a court, some arguing for acquittal and some for guilt, and Daniel's decree of the watchers is the same court. The word he uses for it is pamalya shel ma'alah, the household above, from the Latin familia — so that the Latin bench's senatus and the rabbinic bench's imperial household are the same borrowed picture of a court, used to opposite ends. That God natal reshut, took leave, from his own retinue is the strongest form of the reading anywhere on this daf, and Rashi will spend his second comment defending it against the obvious objection."
)

add(id="rashi-1-26-minim", work="rashi-gen", author="rashi", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=1090, date_precision="range-1075-1105", place="troyes",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "וּתְשׁוּבַת הַמִּינִים כָּתַב בְּצִדּוֹ, וַיִּבְרָא אֶת הָאָדָם, וְלֹא כָתַב וַיִּבְרְאוּ", "en": "and the refutation of the minim he wrote beside it: 'and he created the human', and he did not write 'and they created'"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": _raw("rashi-gen-1-26.json", "he", 1)[0], "source": "Rashi on Gen 1:26, s.v. נעשה אדם (second comment)", "license": "silbermann", "version": "Rosenbaum–Silbermann, 1929–34"},
    english={"text": _raw("rashi-gen-1-26.json", "en", 1)[0], "translator": "M. Rosenbaum and A. M. Silbermann, 1929–34", "license": "silbermann"},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["refutation-beside-the-claim", "one-authority-singular-verb", "counsel-with-the-household-above"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The objection and the answer, in a hundred words, and it is the whole of this crux compressed. The objection: the angels did not help, and the plural gives the minim room to rebel. The answer is in three parts. Scripture did not refrain, because the point was to teach derekh eretz and the measure of humility, that the great should consult and take leave of the lesser. Had it written e'eseh adam, we should not have learned that he speaks with his court but only with himself — which is Ambrose's non dicit faciam, sed faciamus at Milan, the identical grammatical observation put to the identical use of ruling out a soliloquy, and to the opposite conclusion about who is being addressed. And the refutation of the minim was written beside it: va-yivra et ha-adam, and he did not write va-yivre'u, which is R. Simlai's rule and answer from Bereshit Rabbah 8:9. Rashi at Troyes about 1090 is thus doing openly what no Latin witness on this daf does: he names the risk the verse carries, weighs it against what the verse teaches, and decides that the teaching is worth the opening. Whether the minim he has in mind are the Christians of northern France is not said here and is not needed: the grammatical opening is the same one Abelard will exploit at the Paraclete forty years later."
)

