add(id="ibn-ezra-1-26-naaseh", work="ibn-ezra-gen", author="ibn-ezra", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=1155, date_precision="circa", place="lucca",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "וְאֵלֶּה הָעֵדִים עֵדֵי שֶׁקֶר הֵם", "en": "and these witnesses are false witnesses"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": hcut(_ie26_he, "ואמר במלת נעשה, ואם היא לשון רבים",
                                         "אנחנו נתעסק בו, ולא המים והארץ", "K3 ibn-ezra-26 he"),
              "source": "Ibn Ezra on Gen 1:26, s.v. נעשה אדם", "license": "pd", "version": "Piotrkow, 1907–1911"},
    english={"text": ecut(_ie26_en, "And he said [further] that the word na’aseh",
                          "WE will busy ourselves with him, not the water and the earth.", "K3 ibn-ezra-26 en"),
             "translator": "Sefaria Community Translation", "license": "cc0"},
    tradents=["saadia-gaon"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["plural-of-majesty-refused", "faciamus-to-the-angels"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The finding of this session, and it is a correction to what the daf would otherwise say about Ibn Ezra. At Gen 1:1 he explains the plural Elohim as the honorific plural — every language honours the great with a plural, as adonim and be'alim are plural for one master, and as a king in Arabic speaks of himself in the plural (see `ibn-ezra-1-1-elohim`). Here, on the plural verb, he is offered the same explanation by Saadia Gaon — na'aseh is the manner of royal speech, with Gen 29:27, Dan 2:36 and Num 22:6 for proofs — and he demolishes it: ve-elleh ha-edim edei sheker hem, and these witnesses are false witnesses. Each proof text is parsed away, nitnah as a nifal and not a first person plural, nakkeh bo as Balak and his camp or else an infinitive; R. Moshe ha-Kohen is told he erred in his book; and Daniel could not have used a plural of grandeur to the king of kings. Then his own answer: God said to the angels, let us make a human — we shall occupy ourselves with him, and not the water and the earth, which had brought forth the plants and the animals. So the grammarian who takes the plural noun to be empty takes the plural verb to be full, and full in the way the Latin bench most objects to. The two positions are not inconsistent — a plural of honour is a fact about nouns, and na'aseh has a real subject — but only a reader who has both pages can see that Ibn Ezra's answer to this crux is the angel reading."
)

