add(id="ramban-1-26-naaseh", work="ramban-gen", author="ramban", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=1267, date_precision="range-1263-1270", place="girona",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "אֲנִי וְהָאָרֶץ הַנִּזְכֶּרֶת נַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם", "en": "I and the aforementioned earth, let us make a human"},
    original={"lang": "he", "text": hcut(_ram26_he, "וְהַפְּשָׁט הַנָּכוֹן בְּמִלַּת",
                                         "וְהוּא הַנִּרְאֶה מִכָּל מָה שֶׁחָשְׁבוּ בּוֹ.", "K3 ramban-26 he"),
              "source": "Ramban on Gen 1:26, s.v. נעשה אדם", "license": "sefaria-vocalized", "version": "Sefaria 'Vocalized Edition'"},
    english={"text": ecut(_ram26_en, "The correct explanation of na’aseh",
                          "is the most acceptable of all interpretations that have been advanced concerning it.", "K3 ramban-26 en"),
             "translator": "Charles B. Chavel, 1971–76", "license": "chavel-ramban"},
    tradents=["r-yosef-kimchi"],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["i-and-the-earth"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "A fourth answer, and the only one on either bench that keeps the plural literal and puts a creature in it without putting an angel in it. Ramban argues from the order of the chapter: God created something from nothing on the first day only, and afterwards formed and made from what he had created, so that the waters were told to swarm and the earth to bring forth. At the human the formula changes because the two agents are named together — ani ve-ha-aretz ha-nizkeret na'aseh adam, I and the aforementioned earth, let us make a human — the earth to bring forth the body from its elements as it did for the beasts, and the Holy One to give the spirit, as Gen 2:7 then says twice over. Be-tzalmenu ki-dmutenu follows exactly: the human will resemble both, his body the earth he was taken from and his spirit the beings above. He credits the reading to R. Yosef Kimḥi and calls it the most acceptable of all that have been thought about the verse — a phrase that quietly sets aside Rashi's angels, which he does not name here. Ramban's answer at Gen 1:1 was that Elohim is el hem, the force of all the forces, with a secret not disclosed; his answer here needs no secret at all, and no plurality in God whatever."
)

add(id="targ-onk-1-26", work="targ-onk", author="onkelos", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=200, date_precision="range-100-300", place="palestine",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "וַאֲמַר יְיָ נַעֲבִיד אֱינָשָׁא", "en": "And the LORD said, let us make a human"},
    original={"lang": "arc", "text": _raw("targ-onk-1-26.json", "he")[0], "source": "Targum Onkelos, Gen 1:26", "license": "pd", "version": "Sefaria 'Onkelos Genesis'"},
    english={"text": "And the LORD said: let us make a human in our image, as our likeness; and let them rule over the fish of the sea and over the bird of the heavens and over the cattle and over all the earth and over every creeping thing that creeps upon the earth.", **DRAFT},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["translation"],
    answers=["one-authority-singular-verb"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "Evidence by omission, and it only reads as evidence next to the same targum's Gen 1:1. There Onkelos writes YY for Elohim, the Tetragrammaton, which is not plural in form, so that a reader of the Aramaic cannot raise the question of two authorities from the noun at all (see `targ-onk-1-1`). Here the plural verb is left standing: na'avid enasha be-tzalmana ki-dmutana — let us make a human in our image, as our likeness — with nothing added, nobody addressed, and no angels. The one difficulty the Aramaic could have removed as easily as the other, and did not. Whatever governed Onkelos's hand at Gen 1:1, it was not a general policy of smoothing plurals out of the creation account; and Pseudo-Jonathan, working on the same verse in the same language, does add the angels in so many words."
)

add(id="targ-psj-1-26", work="targ-psj", author="targum-pseudo-jonathan", tradition="rabbinic",
    date=750, date_precision="range-600-800", place="palestine",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.2"}, lemma={"he": "וַאֲמַר יְיָ לְמַלְאָכַיָא דִמְשַׁמְשִׁין קוֹמוֹי", "en": "And the LORD said to the angels who minister before him"},
    original={"lang": "arc", "text": _raw("targ-psj-1-26.json", "he")[0], "source": "Targum Pseudo-Jonathan, Gen 1:26", "license": "pd", "version": "Sefaria 'Targum Jonathan on Genesis'"},
    english={"text": _raw("targ-psj-1-26.json", "en")[0], "translator": "J. W. Etheridge, 1862", "license": "etheridge"},
    tradents=[],
    cruxes=["elohim-and-trinity"], senses=["translation"],
    answers=["faciamus-to-the-angels"],
    notes=_ANCHOR26 + "The angel reading written into the text of Scripture itself, which is as far as the rabbinic answer to this crux can be pushed. Where the Hebrew has 'and God said, let us make', Pseudo-Jonathan has 'and the LORD said to the angels who minister before him, who were created on the second day of the creation of the world, let us make a human in our image, in our likeness'. Two additions, and the second is doing work: dating the angels to the second day forestalls the objection that they might have been co-creators, since they were made after the beginning and are therefore creatures like the rest. The Glossa Ordinaria at PL 113:114D says ad angelos dixisse intelligitur — he is understood to have said it to the angels — of the plural at Babel, and cross-refers to faciamus hominem; a Latin reader of the twelfth century and an Aramaic reader of the eighth are looking at the same plural and reaching for the same hearers. That the targum puts it in the biblical line rather than in a gloss is why Basil could say ferunt enim, quod angelis dixerit and be reporting rather than caricaturing."
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- threads (K3, Gen 1:26 block)
THREADS.extend([
 E("t-k3-26", "basil-hex-1-26-judaei", "targ-psj-1-26", "contests", "Basil reports the position and Pseudo-Jonathan is the position. 'Ferunt enim, quod angelis dixerit: Faciamus hominem' — for they say that he said it to the angels — is answered as a Judaica fictio; and the targum reads, in the biblical line itself, 'and the LORD said to the angels who minister before him … let us make a human in our image'. Basil's argument against it is the image clause: is there one image of God and of the angels? The targum has already met that by dating the angels to the second day, so that they are creatures and no rival makers. Neither text has the other, and the caricature turns out to be a quotation."),
 E("t-k3-27", "basil-hex-1-26-judaei", "br-8-9", "contests", "The same grammatical proof on both benches, aimed in opposite directions, and each aimed at the other's people. Basil: 'Non enim dixit ad imagines nostras, sicut et Scriptura testatur: Fecit Deus, inquit, hominem: non fecerunt' — God made the human, they did not make — used to show that the plural is of persons and not of makers. R. Simlai, asked by the minim how many divinities created the world: 'va-yivre'u Elohim et ha-adam be-tzalmeihem is not said, but va-yivra Elohim et ha-adam be-tzalmo.' One verse, one observation about the number of the verb, two disputations, and each bench is arguing with the other's readers."),
 E("t-k3-28", "ambrose-hex-6-7-40", "basil-hex-1-26-judaei", "transmits", "Ambrose is working through Basil at Milan and keeps the order of the questions: to whom does he say it — not to himself, because he does not say faciam; not to the angels, because a servant is no partner in the master's work; therefore to the Son. What he adds is the second front: 'etiamsi Judaei nolint; etiamsi Ariani repugnent', so that the Jewish reading of the plural and the Arian subordination of the Son are refuted by one argument, and whoever denies the Son a share in the working ends by making a gift of it to servuli."),
 E("t-k3-29", "ambrose-hex-6-7-40", "rashi-1-26-minim", "parallel", "The same grammatical fact, the same use made of it, and opposite conclusions about the hearer. Ambrose: 'Cui dicit? Non sibi utique, quia non dicit, faciam, sed faciamus' — he cannot be speaking to himself, because the verb is not singular. Rashi: 'had it written e'eseh adam, we should not have learned that he speaks with his court but with himself.' Both rule out the soliloquy from the form of the verb; Ambrose concludes that the hearer is the Son, Rashi that it is the household above. Milan about 387 and Troyes about 1090, with no possible contact, reasoning identically to incompatible ends."),
 E("t-k3-30", "aug-gnl-imp-16-61", "ambrose-hex-6-7-40", "contests", "Augustine takes away the answer Ambrose had just given, and on grammatical grounds. If the Father were speaking to the Son, then 'ad imaginem nostram' would have to be 'ad imaginem tuam' or 'ad imaginem meam'; and if the plural covered the Father and the Son only, 'a qua pluralitate Spiritum sanctum separare quis audeat?' So the plural is not a conversation between two persons but the Trinity speaking of itself, and the human is made to the image of the whole. Comestor will print both readings side by side eight hundred years later without noticing that the second was written to exclude the first."),
 E("t-k3-31", "aug-gnl-3-19-29", "aug-gnl-imp-16-61", "echoes", "The same reading twenty years on, with the polemic gone and one sentence added that the whole Latin bench will quote: it is not a matter of indifference that elsewhere Scripture says dixit Deus, fiat, and here dixit Deus, faciamus. The unfinished commentary argued from ad imaginem nostram; the finished one argues from the change of formula, which is a claim about how the chapter is written rather than about what the words mean, and is therefore the version that can be abridged into a gloss."),
 E("t-k3-32", "aug-civ-16-6", "b-sanh-38b-tshuva", "parallel", "The strongest structural parallel on the site, and neither side can have known the other. R. Yoḥanan's rule at Sanhedrin 38b lists the places where the minim broke loose with the refutation beside each, and the first two are Gen 1:26, answered by the singular of Gen 1:27, and Gen 11:7 — havah nerdah, come let us go down — answered by the singular of Gen 11:5. Augustine's chapter is headed 'qualis intelligenda sit esse locutio, qua Deus Angelis loquitur' and takes up the same two verses in the same order: at Gen 1:26 the angels are excluded, because ad imaginem nostram forbids them, and 'non dixit, Fecerunt dii'; at Gen 11:7, where there is no image clause, 'si aliquid esset, quod Angelos prohiberet intelligi' — and nothing is, so the angels stand. Two benches select the same pair out of the Pentateuch by the same criterion, a plural verb of divine speech, and apply the same test, the singular that follows. They disagree only about what the plural was doing there."),
 E("t-k3-33", "glossa-11-7-angeli", "b-sanh-38b-tshuva", "parallel", "The Gloss joins Gen 11:7 to Gen 1:26 and answers both with their singulars — 'Ad angelos dixisse intelligitur; vel secundum illud: Faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram. Nam singulari numero subjunctum est: et divisit eos, sicut ibi: Fecit Deus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem suam' — which is R. Yoḥanan's list and R. Yoḥanan's test, in a Latin schoolbook, with the conclusion left open. It is the one place in the Gloss where the angel reading is printed as the plain sense, and it is printed at the verse where the argument from the image cannot be run."),
 E("t-k3-34", "glossa-11-7-angeli", "aug-civ-16-6", "cites", "The gloss is Augustine's chapter reduced to its verdict. De civitate Dei XVI.6 grants the angels at Babel because nothing prevents them, having refused them at Gen 1:26 because of the image; the Gloss keeps the grant, keeps the cross-reference to faciamus hominem, and drops the reason for the difference — so that a twelfth-century reader meets the angel reading of one plural without the argument that had excluded it from the other."),
 E("t-k3-35", "glossa-1-26", "aug-gnl-3-19-29", "cites", "The standard gloss on Gen 1:26 is De Genesi ad litteram III.19 printed under Augustine's name and cut to its two moves: the plural intimates the persons, the singular of v. 27 gives the unity of the deity. What is cut is the argument — Augustine's 'alioquin non vere dictum est, ad imaginem nostram, si ad Patris solius, aut Filii solius imaginem factus est homo' becomes a bare 'non enim vere diceretur ad imaginem nostram'. This is the page on which Hugh, Abelard and Comestor all begin."),
 E("t-k3-36", "bede-gen-1-26", "basil-hex-1-26-judaei", "transmits", "Bede is copying the Latin Basil and keeping the sentence while dropping the fight. 'Donec is qui doceretur non erat, in profundo fuit abdita praedicatio Deitatis; ubi vero coepit hominis exspectari creatio, revelata est fides' stands word for word in both. What Bede removes is every Jew in the passage, and what he puts in its place is a reason: it could not be said to angels, 'quia nulla prorsus ratio sinit ut Dei et angelorum unam esse eamdemque imaginem sive similitudinem credamus'. The argument survives the polemic, and it is the argument, not the polemic, that Rabanus and Angelomus then copy from Bede."),
 E("t-k3-37", "alcuin-int-36-37", "br-8-8", "parallel", "Both take the change of formula to be about the standing of the creature rather than about God, and they draw opposite morals from it. Alcuin: why of the human alone is it said faciamus? — so that, a rational creature being founded, it might be seen to be made with counsel, 'et ut ejus nobilitas ostenderetur'. Bereshit Rabbah: the plural was worth the opening it gives the minim because a great man who will not ask leave of a lesser must learn from his Creator, who consulted the ministering angels. Nobility on one bench, humility on the other, out of the same observation that this verse and no other has a consultative form."),
 E("t-k3-38", "wigbod-gen-1-26", "aug-gnl-3-19-29", "transmits", "Augustine's sentence cut in half and given to two speakers, D and M, so that the observation is a pupil's and the doctrine a master's, and headed AMBROSIUS. One word changes and it matters: Augustine wrote 'ad imaginem Dei' and argued for a page why it is not 'ad imaginem Filii'; Wigbod writes 'ad imaginem suam', which settles the question by removing it."),
 E("t-k3-39", "remigius-gen-1-26", "br-8-3", "parallel", "The same question in one word each. Remigius: 'quasi quodam concilio dicit: Faciamus' — as though by a kind of council he says, let us make. Bereshit Rabbah: 'be-mi nimlakh', with whom did he take counsel. Both take the change of formula to imply a consultation and then have to say with whom; and Remigius adds the line that shows the Latin bench knew there was more than one answer available — 'quod, licet quidam ad angelos referendum dicant, melius tamen ad sanctam Trinitatem refertur'. Some do refer it to the angels, at Auxerre about 890, and he neither names them nor refutes them."),
 E("t-k3-40", "rupert-gen-1-26-consilium", "br-8-3", "parallel", "Bereshit Rabbah 8:3 sets out two extremes and does not choose: R. Yehoshua in R. Levi's name has God consult the work of heaven and earth, with a parable of a king and his two sanqlitin — Greek synklētikoi, senators — and R. Ami has him take counsel be-libbo, with his own heart. Rupert names both in Latin and chooses: the counsel of the Trinity was 'non tam senatu quam soliloquio venerando … habitum'. The Roman word for the one and the Augustinian word for the other, six hundred years later, at Liège, with no contact; and the soliloquy that proves for R. Ami that nobody else was present proves for Rupert that three persons were."),
 E("t-k3-41", "abelard-hex-1-26-faciamus", "br-8-3", "parallel", "R. Ami's answer with a bibliography. 'Quasi ergo aliquis secum loquens se et rationem suam quasi duo constituit cum eam consulit, sicut Boetius in libro De consolatione Philosophiae, vel Augustinus in libro Soliloquiorum' — a man who consults his own reason sets up himself and his reason as two. That is be-libbo nimlakh, he took counsel with his heart, made into a philosophical model; and where the midrash uses the inward counsel to show that no second party was present, Abelard uses it to show how a real plurality speaks with one mouth."),
 E("t-k3-42", "abelard-hex-1-26-faciamus", "abelard-hex-1-1-eloim", "echoes", "One man, one argument, two verses, and the pair is the whole of this crux. At Gen 1:1 the evidence is a Hebrew noun plural in form beside a singular verb — 'unde autem dictum est Eloim creavit, non creaverunt?' At Gen 1:26 it is a Latin verb plural in form beside the singular of v. 27 — 'sed cur pluraliter dicitur: faciamus hominem ad imaginem nostram, si nulla prorsus pluralitas in Deo sit … et creavit Deus hominem'. The inference is identical both times, the plural for the persons and the singular for the one creator, and both times it is addressed to Jews by name: dicant Judaei si possunt."),
 E("t-k3-43", "abelard-hex-1-26-faciamus", "rashi-1-26-minim", "contests", "The opening and the man who takes it, forty years apart and about two hundred miles. Rashi grants that na'aseh gives the minim room to rebel, and keeps it anyway for the sake of the lesson in humility, writing the refutation beside it: va-yivra and not va-yivre'u. Abelard walks into precisely that opening — 'dicant Judaei si possunt, vel nobiscum fateantur in una Divinitatis essentia pluralitatem personarum' — and takes Rashi's own refutation, the singular of v. 27, as the second half of his proof rather than as an answer to it. Neither has read the other and the argument fits together exactly."),
 E("t-k3-44", "hugh-adnot-1-26", "br-8-8", "parallel", "The identical moral drawn from the identical difficulty, at St Victor about 1130 and in Galilee seven hundred years earlier. Hugh: God brings in counsel 'ut nos cautos reddat, ne dedignemur consilium accipere et ab aequalibus et a minoribus; cum ipse ad angelos ita loquatur'. Bereshit Rabbah: if a great man will not seek leave from one lesser than himself, let him learn from his Creator, who consulted the ministering angels when he came to make a human. Both make the verse a lesson in taking advice downwards, and both ground the lesson in the angels — which is the one thing about this verse the rest of the Latin bench will not concede."),
 E("t-k3-45", "hugh-adnot-1-26", "targ-psj-1-26", "parallel", "Hugh goes further than the targum. Pseudo-Jonathan has God address the ministering angels and stops there, and guards the point by dating them to the second day so that they cannot be co-creators. Hugh writes 'ad angelos ita loquatur, quorum ministerio forsitan formatum est corpus hominis' — by whose ministry perhaps the human body was formed — which grants the angels a hand in the work itself. That is the position Basil calls a Jewish fiction, Ambrose refuses because a servant is no partner, and Bruno says would make many creators; it is in a Victorine schoolbook, offered before the Trinitarian reading and only then set aside with vel quod melius est."),
 E("t-k3-46", "hugh-adnot-1-26", "remigius-gen-1-26", "echoes", "The same two-reading structure two hundred and forty years apart, and the interval shows the angel reading losing ground without ever being refuted. Remigius: some refer it to the angels, but it is better referred to the holy Trinity. Hugh: he speaks to the angels, by whose ministry perhaps the body was formed — or, what is better, take it as the counsel of the Trinity. Both put the angels first and prefer the Trinity comparatively, neither argues against the angels, and by Comestor's Historia scholastica forty years after Hugh the option is simply not mentioned."),
 E("t-k3-47", "comestor-hs-1-26", "aug-gnl-imp-16-61", "contests", "The handbook prints as an alternative the reading Augustine wrote to exclude. 'Et loquitur Pater ad Filium, et Spiritum sanctum. Vel est quasi communis vox trium personarum' — the first is Ambrose's and Basil's, the second is Augustine's, and Augustine's objection to the first is that ad imaginem nostram would then have to be tuam or meam. Comestor ranks neither and argues for neither. What a priest examined on the Historia scholastica about 1170 knows about this verse is that there are two acceptable answers and no reason to prefer either."),
 E("t-k3-48", "br-8-9", "br-1-7", "echoes", "The same proof twice in the same compilation, once as exegesis and once as a disputation. R. Yitzḥak at 1:7 observes that the plural-looking Elohim takes singular verbs — not vaydaberu but vaydaber, not bare'u but bara — and concludes that no creature may say two authorities created the world. R. Simlai at 8:9 is asked by the minim how many divinities created the world and answers with the same verb: baru Elohim is not written here but bara Elohim. What 8:9 adds is the occasion. The grammatical observation this crux is built on is, in its own tradition, an answer given to opponents who had asked the question out loud."),
 E("t-k3-49", "br-8-9", "br-8-8", "echoes", "Two paragraphs, one anxiety, and they name it the same way: pitchon peh la-minim, an opening for the minim. At 8:8 Moses sees the opening while he is writing the verse and is told to write it anyway; at 8:9 the minim walk through it and are met at the door. R. Simlai's rule states the policy the earlier paragraph enacted — everywhere you find an opening for the minim, you find the refutation beside it — so that the danger and its answer are treated as a designed pair rather than as an accident to be explained away."),
 E("t-k3-50", "b-sanh-38b-tshuva", "br-8-9", "echoes", "R. Simlai's rule become R. Yoḥanan's, with the disputation dropped and the list extended. In the midrash the rule is said between two exchanges and is illustrated by them; in the Bavli it is stated flat and followed by three verse-pairs, Gen 1:26 with 1:27, Gen 11:7 with 11:5, Gen 35:7 with 35:3. The verb hardens too: she'alu ha-minim, the minim asked, becomes kol makom she-pakru ha-minim, everywhere the minim broke loose."),
 E("t-k3-51", "b-sanh-38b-malachim", "br-8-3", "echoes", "Bereshit Rabbah asks with whom God took counsel and gives four answers without choosing; the Bavli tells the story that answers it and lets the answer cost something. Rav Yehuda in Rav's name has God create a company of ministering angels and put the verse to them as a question — retzonkhem na'aseh adam be-tzalmenu — and burn them when they object, and the second company after them, until the third says that the world is his and he may do as he wishes. The consultation is real, in the words of the verse; the partnership is destroyed in the telling."),
 E("t-k3-52", "rashi-1-26-anvetanuto", "b-sanh-38b-malachim", "cites", "Rashi's first comment is the Bavli's story turned into a doctrine of God, and he ends by quoting it: 'there are in the heavens beings after my likeness; if there will not be on earth also beings after my likeness, there will be envy among the beings that I have created.' What the daf leaves as a narrative — the angels object, they are burnt — Rashi makes a habit: this is how God judges kings too, as Micaiah saw, and as Daniel's decree of the watchers shows. The word for the court is pamalya shel ma'alah, from the Latin familia."),
 E("t-k3-53", "rashi-1-26-minim", "br-8-9", "cites", "Rashi ends the second comment with R. Simlai's answer verbatim in Hebrew — 'and the refutation of the minim he wrote beside it, va-yivra et ha-adam, and he did not write va-yivre'u' — and with it he takes over the midrash's whole posture: name the risk, keep the verse, point to the answer alongside. The one thing he does not take over is R. Simlai's caution. Where the midrash has the disciples object that the minim were pushed off with a reed, Rashi lets the grammatical answer stand as the answer."),
 E("t-k3-54", "ibn-ezra-1-26-naaseh", "ibn-ezra-1-1-elohim", "contests", "One grammarian, two plurals, two incompatible accounts, and the daf can only show it because both pages are here. At Gen 1:1 the plural noun Elohim is the honorific plural and means nothing more: every language honours the great with a plural, as adonim and be'alim are plural for one master and as a king in Arabic speaks of himself in the plural. At Gen 1:26 Saadia Gaon offers him the identical explanation for the plural verb — na'aseh is the manner of royal speech — and he calls the proof texts false witnesses, parses every one of them away, and puts a real hearer in their place: God said to the angels, let us make a human, we shall occupy ourselves with him and not the water and the earth. The plural of majesty is a fact about nouns and does not reach a verb with a subject; but the reader who has only the Gen 1:1 page would take Ibn Ezra's answer to this crux to be the opposite of what it is."),
 E("t-k3-55", "ibn-ezra-1-26-naaseh", "rashi-1-26-anvetanuto", "parallel", "The two great northern and southern commentators arrive at the same hearer by different routes and with different tempers. Rashi gets to the angels from the humility of God and the court of 1 Kgs 22, and has to defend the reading against the minim in his next comment. Ibn Ezra gets to them by elimination: the earth and the waters brought forth the plants and the animals, and at the human God says we shall occupy ourselves with him and not they. No court, no humility, no polemic, and no acknowledgement that anybody reads the verse otherwise."),
 E("t-k3-56", "ramban-1-26-naaseh", "ibn-ezra-1-26-naaseh", "contests", "Ramban keeps the plural literal and takes the angels out of it. 'Ani ve-ha-aretz ha-nizkeret na'aseh adam' — I and the aforementioned earth, let us make a human: the earth to bring forth the body from its elements as it did for the beasts, and God to give the spirit, exactly as Gen 2:7 then describes in two clauses. The plural has a second party and the second party is the ground. He credits R. Yosef Kimḥi and calls it the most acceptable of all that have been thought about the verse, which is how he sets aside both the angels of Ibn Ezra and Rashi and the plural of majesty of Saadia without arguing with any of them."),
 E("t-k3-57", "ramban-1-26-naaseh", "ramban-1-1-elohim", "echoes", "The same man refusing, twice, to let a plural be either empty or personal, and needing a secret only once. At Gen 1:1 Elohim is a compound, el hem, the force of all the forces, and 'a secret will yet be disclosed in connection with this'. At Gen 1:26 the plural verb has a plain second subject, the earth, and nothing is withheld — the mystery is moved to the following verse, where 'the way of truth in this verse will be known to him who understands'. Of the four answers this crux collects, Ramban's is the only one that makes the plural real without making it a plurality in God or a company of angels."),
 E("t-k3-58", "targ-onk-1-26", "targ-onk-1-1", "contests", "The same targum, the same difficulty, opposite hands. At Gen 1:1 Onkelos writes YY for Elohim, so that the plural-looking noun disappears and no reader of the Aramaic could raise the question of two authorities from it. At Gen 1:26 he leaves the plural verb standing — na'avid enasha be-tzalmana — and adds nothing, addresses nobody, and supplies no angels. Whatever governed the first choice, it was not a policy of removing plurals from the creation account, and the contrast is the best evidence on the daf that the Gen 1:1 substitution was about the divine name rather than about number."),
 E("t-k3-59", "targ-psj-1-26", "targ-onk-1-26", "contests", "Two Aramaic versions of one verse, one adding the hearers and one refusing to. Pseudo-Jonathan: 'and the LORD said to the angels who minister before him, who were created on the second day of the creation of the world, let us make a human in our image, in our likeness.' Onkelos: 'and the LORD said, let us make a human in our image, as our likeness.' The later targum answers the question the earlier one left open, and guards its answer by dating the angels after the beginning so that they cannot be makers."),
 E("t-k3-60", "targ-psj-1-26", "glossa-11-7-angeli", "parallel", "An Aramaic targum of the eighth century and a Latin schoolbook of the twelfth put the same hearers behind the same kind of plural. 'Va-amar YY le-malakhaya di-meshamshin qomoi' — and the LORD said to the angels who minister before him. 'Ad angelos dixisse intelligitur' — he is understood to have said it to the angels. Neither argues for it; both simply state it as what the verse means. The Gloss says it at Gen 11:7 and cross-refers to Gen 1:26; the targum says it at Gen 1:26 outright."),
 E("t-k3-61", "bruno-gen-1-1c", "targ-psj-1-26", "contests", "Bruno's rhetorical question has a literal answer in a book he could not read. 'Quos ad hominem creandum invitabat, dicens: Faciamus hominem?' — whom was he inviting to create the human? — and his answer is that it cannot be the angels, since otherwise there would be not one creator but many. Pseudo-Jonathan names them: the angels who minister before him, created on the second day. The targum has already conceded Bruno's premise, that no creature may be a maker, and secured it by the date; Bruno takes the reading to be refuted by the premise alone."),
 E("t-k3-62", "aug-civ-16-6", "hugh-adnot-1-26", "contests", "Augustine allows the angels one plural and not the other, and gives the reason; Hugh allows them both, and takes the reason back. De civitate Dei XVI.6 refuses them at Gen 1:26 because ad imaginem nostram forbids it, and grants them at Gen 11:7 because nothing prevents it. Hugh writes 'cum ipse ad angelos ita loquatur, quorum ministerio forsitan formatum est corpus hominis' at Gen 1:26 itself, where Augustine's objection stands unanswered, and then prefers the Trinity for a different reason altogether — that it is better, not that the angels are excluded."),
])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new answers / persons (Gen 1:26)
ANSWERS.update({
 "faciamus-is-the-trinity": {"label": "'Let us make' is the Trinity", "gloss": "The formula changes at this one verse — everywhere else 'let it be', here 'let us make' — to intimate a plurality of persons; the singular of v. 27 then gives the unity of the deity (Augustine, Bede, Alcuin, Wigbod, Remigius, the Glossa, Rupert, Abelard, Hugh, Comestor)."},
 "faciamus-to-the-son": {"label": "The Father speaks to the Son", "gloss": "Not to himself, since the verb is not 'I shall make', and not to servants: the address is to the Son, who is the image (Basil, Ambrose, Comestor; refused by Augustine, since 'to our image' would then have to be 'to yours')."},
 "faciamus-to-the-angels": {"label": "He said it to the angels", "gloss": "God took counsel with, or spoke to, the ministering angels — stated outright by Pseudo-Jonathan, argued by Rashi and Ibn Ezra, reported as an option by Remigius, taught with a moral by Hugh, printed at Gen 11:7 by the Glossa, and called a Jewish fiction by Basil."},
 "image-of-the-whole-trinity": {"label": "To the image of the Trinity itself", "gloss": "Not to the image of the Father alone, or the Son alone, or the Spirit alone — and who would dare separate the Holy Spirit from that plurality? (Augustine, and the Gloss after him)."},
 "counsel-with-the-household-above": {"label": "He took counsel with the household above", "gloss": "Be-mi nimlakh — with the ministering angels, the court that stands to right and left, out of the humility of the Holy One (Bereshit Rabbah 8:3 and 8:8, b. Sanhedrin 38b, Rashi)."},
 "consilium-not-senate": {"label": "A soliloquy, not a senate", "gloss": "The counsel was held inwardly — be-libbo nimlakh, he took counsel with his heart; non tam senatu quam soliloquio — though the rabbinic bench draws from it that no second party was present and the Latin bench that three persons were (R. Ami, Rupert, Abelard)."},
 "refutation-beside-the-claim": {"label": "The refutation is beside the opening", "gloss": "Wherever the verse gives the minim an opening, the answer stands next to it: 'let us make' answered by 'and he created', 'let us go down' by 'and the LORD came down' (R. Simlai, R. Yoḥanan, Rashi)."},
 "dignity-of-the-creature": {"label": "The consultative form marks the creature's standing", "gloss": "A rational creature is made with counsel, so that its nobility may be shown — or, on the other bench, so that the great may learn to ask leave of the lesser (Alcuin, Bede, Hugh, Abelard; Bereshit Rabbah 8:8)."},
 "plural-of-majesty-refused": {"label": "Not a plural of majesty", "gloss": "Saadia's reading of na'aseh as royal speech is refused and its proof texts parsed away one by one — 'these witnesses are false witnesses' (Ibn Ezra, against the Gaon, and against his own account of the plural noun at Gen 1:1)."},
 "i-and-the-earth": {"label": "I and the earth", "gloss": "The plural has a second subject and it is the ground: the earth to bring forth the body from its elements, God to give the spirit (Ramban, crediting R. Yosef Kimḥi)."},
})

